1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Here is the simple idea: good clips are usually found before editing begins.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Instead of hunting through a full recording, start with the transcript.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Look for the moment where the speaker names a problem in plain language.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
That line becomes the hook because it gives viewers a reason to care.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
The next few lines should explain the idea without needing extra context.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
If the takeaway can stand alone, it is already closer to a useful short.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Now score the clip for clarity, novelty, emotion, and effort to edit.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
A strong candidate is not just exciting; it is specific enough to finish.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
For this demo, ClipOps turns those timestamps into reviewable candidates.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Nothing is posted anywhere; the publishing queue is intentionally simulated.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The reviewer can approve, reject, or request edits before a clip moves on.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
That human checkpoint keeps the workflow useful instead of fully automatic.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
After approval, mock performance data helps compare different hook styles.

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The dashboard then shows simulated views, engagement, and approval patterns.

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Weekly reports summarize what worked and what should be tested next.

16
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
The expected outcome is not viral certainty; it is a repeatable review loop.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
That loop makes every transcript easier to inspect, segment, and discuss.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
It also creates a clear audit trail for decisions made during content prep.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
By the end, the team has candidates, draft assets, and simulated metrics.

20
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
That is enough for a practical demo without relying on real platform data.
